<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harichavank</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harichavank is a monastery in the village of Harich in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirak">Shirak</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, near Artik. Founded in the 7th century, it was enlarged after 1201, when the Zakarid princes Zakare and Ivane acquired it and built the large Church of the Holy Mother of God. It was a major centre of learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copying in medieval Armenia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harichavank.jpg" alt="Harichavank" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liveon001 © Travis K. Witt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harichavank_Rear.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Excavations in 1966 showed that Harich existed in the 2nd century BCE and was a fortress town. The monastery's founding date is unknown, but it was probably in being by the 7th century, when its oldest building, the domed Church of St. Gregory the Enlightener, was raised in the style named after the church of St. Hovhannes at Mastara. The Kipchak Turks occupied and altered the monastery from 1120 to 1191, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakarids">Zakarids</a> restored its traditional decoration after they regained power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Cathedral of the Holy Mother of God dominates the complex. It was ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakare-mkhargrdzeli">Zakare Zakarian</a>, commander-in-chief, who with his brother Ivane ruled Eastern Armenia, after he bought Harich from a family of the Pahlavuni dynasty. It is a cruciform church with two-storey sacristies in each arm and a tall twenty-sided drum, a design later repeated at Gandzasar. A relief on the eastern facade shows the two brothers holding a model of the church; it was covered in 1895 by a marble plaque of the Madonna and came to light again when the plaque was removed.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakarids">Zakarids</a>, Vahram, added the narthex before 1219. The larger gavit, built of stones up to 3.5 metres long, rests on four pillars under a stalactite ceiling. South of the monastery stands the Hermitage Chapel on a cliff, and the cemetery holds the ruins of a 5th-century basilica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The princes' privileges made Harichavank a large centre of learning, known for its school and scriptorium. In 1850 it became the summer residence of the Katholikos of Echmiadzin, and the grounds were extended northwards within walls and towers, with offices, a refectory, a school, a hostel, an inn and stores. Ornamented tombstones of the 5th and 6th centuries from its cemetery are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s State History Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harich Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A wave of building produced monasteries and churches at <b>Harichavank</b>, Haghartsin, Geghard and Ani.</blockquote><a href="/state/zakarid-armenia">Zakarid Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
